theory.intro:
Once a dataset is ordered, three features stand out at a glance: **clusters**, **gaps** and **outliers**. You identify all three **informally**, by looking at how the values are spaced, with no calculation needed.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.9]
\draw[<->] (1.6,0)--(9.6,0);
\foreach \x in {2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9} {\draw (\x,-0.12)--(\x,0.12); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.15) {$\x$};}
\foreach \x/\c in {3/3,4/5,5/4,8/1} {\foreach \k in {1,...,\c} {\fill (\x,{0.35*\k}) circle (2.5pt);}}
\node[font=\small] at (4,2.5) {Cluster};
\node[font=\small] at (8,1.4) {Outlier};
\draw[->] (8,1.15)--(8,0.65);
\draw[<->] (6,-0.75)--(7,-0.75);
\node[font=\small] at (6.5,-1.15) {Gap};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (5.5,-1.75) {Data value};
\node[font=\small] at (5.5,3.1) {Cluster, gap and outlier};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
The largest value is **not** automatically an outlier — it is one only when it sits far from the next value.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.9]
\draw[<->] (1.6,0)--(8.6,0);
\foreach \x in {2,3,4,5,6,7,8} {\draw (\x,-0.12)--(\x,0.12); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.15) {$\x$};}
\foreach \x/\c in {3/2,4/3,5/3,6/2,7/1} {\foreach \k in {1,...,\c} {\fill (\x,{0.35*\k}) circle (2.5pt);}}
\node[font=\small] at (7,1.5) {Largest};
\draw[->] (7,1.25)--(7,0.65);
\node[font=\small] at (5,2.6) {Largest value, but no outlier};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (5,-0.9) {Data value};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A wide spread does not create an outlier: values spaced evenly across a large interval have no value far from the rest.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.8]
\draw[<->] (-0.6,0)--(12.6,0);
\foreach \x in {0,2,4,6,8,10,12} {\draw (\x,-0.12)--(\x,0.12); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.15) {$\x$};}
\foreach \x in {0,2,4,6,8,10,12} {\fill (\x,0.4) circle (2.5pt);}
\node[font=\small] at (6,1.6) {Evenly spread, so no outlier};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (6,-0.9) {Data value};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

